1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,410
Your credit card statement shows ten million dong, with an option to pay a five percent minimum of five hundred thousand.

2
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:07,020
You tap pay five hundred thousand...

3
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:11,000
so why does next month's debt surge to ten million ninety-two thousand five hundred dong?

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Because just two days after you swiped, the issuing bank advanced real cash out of pocket to pay the store.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
The forty-five-day grace period is strictly conditional: it only applies if you settle one hundred percent.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:36,000
On day forty-five, paying five hundred thousand prevents late fees and protects credit scores, but paying less than one hundred percent cancels the forty-five-day grace period retroactively.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:51,000
At an illustrative zero point one percent daily rate, the bank calculates interest backward from day one: four hundred and fifty thousand on the original ten million, plus one hundred and forty-two thousand five hundred on the remaining nine point five million.

8
00:00:51,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Total interest reaches five hundred and ninety-two thousand five hundred dong — exceeding the five hundred thousand you just paid, expanding your new statement balance to ten million ninety-two thousand five hundred dong.

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,250
The minimum payment is an emergency buffer for cash flow pinches.

10
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:12,000
When funds are available, paying one hundred percent of your statement balance is the only way to keep the forty-five-day grace period.
